896:/// Compare two `claude --version` strings as dotted numeric tuples (e.g.
897:/// `"2.1.237"`). `true` only when both parse in that shape and `installed`
898:/// is strictly greater component-wise — an unparseable, equal, or older
899:/// version returns `false` rather than guessing which way a mismatched
900:/// shape should be read.
901:fn is_strictly_newer(installed: &str, running: &str) -> bool {
902:    fn parts(v: &str) -> Option<Vec<u64>> {
903:        v.split('.').map(|p| p.parse::<u64>().ok()).collect()
904:    }
905:    match (parts(installed), parts(running)) {
906:        (Some(installed), Some(running)) => installed > running,
907:        _ => false,
908:    }
909:}
910:
911:/// The installed `claude` version, but only when it is confidently newer
912:/// than the one this coordinator session actually launched with. `None`
913:/// covers "no update available", "can't tell" (no recorded launch version,
914:/// probe failed, either version unparseable) and a downgrade alike — the
915:/// caller must never distinguish those and must never render an "up to
916:/// date" state from this. Deliberately pure — `installed` is resolved by
917:/// the caller (see `probe_installed_claude_version`/[`ClaudeVersionProbe`]),
918:/// so this is directly testable without spawning a real process.
919:pub(crate) fn coordinator_update_available(record: &CoordinatorTmuxRecord, installed: Option<&str>) -> Option<String> {
920:    let running = record.launched_claude_version.as_deref()?;
921:    let installed = installed?;
922:    is_strictly_newer(installed, running).then_some(installed.to_owned())
923:}
